The code statements that lead to the bug are:
ret = idr_alloc(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, args->new_handle, args->new_handle + 1, GFP_NOWAIT);
Reason:
args->new_handle is a u32 from userspace. When it exceeds INT_MAX, it becomes negative when cast to int in idr_alloc's start parameter, triggering a WARN_ON_ONCE because start < 0. The kernel warning can be triggered by userspace.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (args->new_handle > INT_MAX)
    return -EINVAL;
handle = args->new_handle;
...
ret = idr_alloc(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, handle, handle + 1, GFP_NOWAIT);
Reason:
The check rejects any handle > INT_MAX early, preventing the invalid value from reaching idr_alloc. The handle variable is an int, so when assigned from args->new_handle (which is within int range after the check), it is safe. The idr_alloc call now uses handle, which is within valid int range, avoiding the warning.

